    scenario:
      'Your loan-agreement extraction fills 42 fields per document and returns one overall `confidence` score per document. Documents below 0.85 go to reviewers, who must re-check all 42 fields; they handle 260 documents a day and the queue keeps growing. Audits show two problems: most fields in routed documents were already correct, and 70% of the errors that reached production sat in documents scored above 0.9, each with one wrong critical field, usually `interest_rate_margin` or `first_payment_date`, among 41 correct ones. A labeled validation set of 1,500 agreements exists from the original rollout. Documents run from 30 to 120 pages, and the team recently moved to a model with a larger context window.',
    ask: 'Which change would most improve where reviewer attention goes?',
    options: [
      'Raise the routing threshold to 0.92 so that more of the documents that hide a wrong critical field reach reviewers, and ask reviewers to check the critical fields first whenever they open a routed document.',
      'Have the model return a confidence score per field, calibrate a review threshold for each field on the labeled validation set, and send reviewers only the fields below threshold instead of whole documents.',
      'Calibrate the document-level score on the labeled validation set separately for each agreement template, so that each template gets a routing threshold that reflects how often it really contains errors.',
      'Add a second pass in the same conversation in which the model re-checks its own extraction and lowers the document score when it finds a field it is unsure about, before the routing decision is made.',
    ],
    answer: 1,
    why: [
      'One wrong field among 41 correct ones barely moves a document-level score, so a higher threshold mostly adds whole documents that are already correct to an overloaded queue.',
      'Calibrated field-level scores point at the specific values likely to be wrong, such as the margin or the first payment date, so reviewers check a few fields instead of 42 and confident-but-wrong fields get caught.',
      'Per-template calibration makes the score more honest on average, but it is still one number per document: a document with one wrong critical field still looks good, and reviewers still re-check all 42 fields.',
      'A model reviewing its own output in the same session keeps its earlier reasoning and is unlikely to question it, and the result still collapses into one document score that hides which field is wrong.',
    ],
    rule: 'Route review on calibrated field-level confidence, not on one score per document.',
